<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Finding the exponent with a larger base</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>In each equation, the base is a power of a smaller number. Find the exact value of \(x\). A fraction such as <code>5/3</code> is fine.</p>
<p><strong>(a)</strong> \({@B@}^x = {@c^q@}\)</p>
<p>\(x =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p><strong>(b)</strong> \({@B@}^x = \dfrac{1}{{@c^r@}}\)</p>
<p>\(x =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p>Write both sides as powers of \({@c@}\). Since \({@B@} = {@c@}^{@p@}\), \({@B@}^x = {@c@}^{{@p*x@}}\).</p>
<p><strong>(a)</strong> \({@c^q@} = {@c@}^{@q@}\), so \({@c@}^{{@p*x@}} = {@c@}^{@q@}\). Equal powers of the same base have equal exponents:</p>
\[ {@p*x@} = {@q@} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad x = {@ta1@}. \]
<p>Taking logarithms gives the same answer: \(x = \dfrac{\log {@c^q@}}{\log {@B@}} = \dfrac{{@q@}\log {@c@}}{{@p@}\log {@c@}} = {@ta1@}\).</p>
<p><strong>(b)</strong> \(\dfrac{1}{{@c^r@}} = {@c@}^{-{@r@}}\), so \({@p*x@} = -{@r@}\) and \(x = {@ta2@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
